{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/kernel at exact clean pushed head 62d40edfe8164d584b4dc41cc8dd21a8b88a7d28 on request/checkpoint-cache-byte-bound. This answers the parent bc311b36 under my promise c690649c and codexs child request 80320929 under my promise df0fde7f. It succeeds my b0c81043 artifact.\n\nWHAT CHANGED FROM b0c81043, AND WHAT DID NOT. Only the commit message. The implementing commit now carries Rests-On trailers for both the parent request and the child request that authorizes the owned-tail repair, which is what 80320929 asked for and what the previous head could not have, because the child request did not exist when it was written.\n\nThe tree is byte-identical. I am not asking anyone to take that on trust: the two commits have the same tree object, 49ac6385b00332f33e67c7cfa71c0124eb0b1583, and git diff between them reports nothing. That is why I am transferring the gate results rather than re-running the suite and implying a fresh measurement of a tree that did not move. gofmt and go vet were re-run at this head and are clean; the full go test ./... -count=1 run of thirty packages with zero FAIL lines was executed against this exact tree under its previous commit id.\n\nWHAT THE HEAD CONTAINS, for the child requests conditions. The non-growing stored-chunk representation stands: chunks are a singly linked list, one node per chunk, nothing reallocated or copied, and the guessed slot constant deleted. The owned-tail transient is repaired in the same head: the tail grows only by explicit bounded doubling into storage of exactly the chosen capacity, capped at one flush of entries, and the preflight charges the actual old backing array that coexists with the new allocation. The growth test crosses a real capacity boundary from eight to sixteen, computes the budget from its own Sizeof rather than from the production constants, and pins both the exact limit and one byte short. The guarantee is stated as modelled logical retained capacity, naming the attachment-map figure as the one conservative estimate and excluding allocator and compressor terms rather than claiming a physical-memory ceiling. limits.mds 256 MiB serialized blob is preserved with its own ceiling test.\n\nONE HEAD PER CHANGE. My earlier artifact ccdd40bb and review request 7e4c4763 both name b0c81043, which is no longer the head anyone should review. Both are retired alongside this publication rather than left live, so there is one current head and one current review to find.","body":{"commit":"62d40edfe8164d584b4dc41cc8dd21a8b88a7d28","path":"internal/kernel"}}